./research/audit/wave10-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-cex-indicator-of-a-fat-cantor-set-is-not-integrable.result.json:16:  "tail": "VERDICT: CLEAN  \n\nBOUNDARY: The counterexample applies to a nondegenerate interval $[0,1]$ with $0<1$.  The set $S$ is nonempty and nowhere dense.  Every partition subinterval has positive length, so the open interior used in the lower-sum argument is nonempty.  The indicator is bounded and the Darboux sums are well‑defined.  No degenerate or empty case invalidates the reasoning.\n\nSOURCES: Every cited item ([[thm-fat-cantor-set-has-positive-measure]], [[def-nowhere-dense-meager]], [[def-partition-and-refinement]], [[def-darboux-sums]], [[def-darboux-integral]], and the ordinal arithmetic lemmas) was inspected; the exact statements match the proof’s usage.\n\nCHECKED: The title, public claim, every numbered step (1.1–5.1) and the Remarks were examined.  The argument is logically sound, correctly applies the positive‑measure property of the fat Cantor set, and avoids any hidden choice principle."
