./research/audit/wave12-judge.jsonl:9:{"id":"def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":false,"reason":"The restriction claim says f is integrable on every closed subinterval, including a degenerate [c,c]. But this definition defines integrability only for a<b, and the cited subinterval lemma requires c<d; its remarks explicitly leave the degenerate case to the oriented integral convention.","context_sha256":"6a2ab77f8208dca0e1a3c3d26b1d474b3b2bb2863c49edc7c8daa380649263af","item_sha256":"c3c3b00ab208524e52321af57ca99dd448ba8fb826ac84fd3be3e72e598ce017","at":"2026-08-10T02:40:30.250Z"}
./research/audit/wave12-judge.jsonl:57:{"id":"thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":false,"reason":"L10 claims that a product of nonnegative reals is nonnegative, but neither cited item states that: one gives inverse positivity and the other gives absolute-value properties. The fact is unsupported.","context_sha256":"b81430435077369e60146085dd9f57683ef37046d04b35c58268d854b2e3061d","item_sha256":"5e08ebb866f9c022762b859264b75ba3ef8b7cc4b189ac43f0997e0383e163f8","at":"2026-08-10T02:43:39.505Z"}
./research/audit/wave12-judge.jsonl:72:{"id":"thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","keep":false,"reason":"The hypothesis 'f is integrable' is only defined for a<b in the cited definition, but the theorem allows a=b, making the premise ill-formed for a=b. The proof uses oriented integrals, but the definition does not define integrability on a singleton.","context_sha256":"b81430435077369e60146085dd9f57683ef37046d04b35c58268d854b2e3061d","item_sha256":"5e08ebb866f9c022762b859264b75ba3ef8b7cc4b189ac43f0997e0383e163f8","at":"2026-08-10T02:46:51.356Z"}
./research/audit/wave12-judge.jsonl:74:{"id":"def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","keep":true,"reason":"The definition is mathematically correct and all cited items are accurately used. The componentwise equivalence is properly derived, and the integral definition is consistent. No false claims or missing citations were found in the core content.","context_sha256":"6a2ab77f8208dca0e1a3c3d26b1d474b3b2bb2863c49edc7c8daa380649263af","item_sha256":"c3c3b00ab208524e52321af57ca99dd448ba8fb826ac84fd3be3e72e598ce017","at":"2026-08-10T02:47:20.360Z"}
