items/thm-mean-value-inequality.md-115-- **What the auxiliary function buys.** The scalar mean value theorem produces a point at which one real function has its average slope. Applying it to $\varphi = \langle u, f\rangle$ for the **particular** $u = f(b)-f(a)$ turns that into a statement about $f$, at the cost of the equality becoming an inequality. The loss is not an artefact of the proof: the equality form is genuinely false for $m \ge 2$, and the companion page's curve $t \mapsto (t^{2},t^{3})$ on $[0,1]$ is a differentiable witness.
items/thm-mean-value-inequality.md-116-
items/thm-mean-value-inequality.md-117-- **The bound is sharp.** No constant smaller than $\sup\lVert f'\rVert_2$ works in general; the companion page exhibits a curve for which the inequality is an equality.
items/thm-mean-value-inequality.md-118-
items/thm-mean-value-inequality.md:119:- **The case split at $u = 0$ is where the statement would otherwise be incomplete**, exactly as in [[thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral]]. When $f(b) = f(a)$ the left-hand side is $0$ and nothing is divided.
items/thm-mean-value-inequality.md-120-
items/thm-mean-value-inequality.md-121-- **$M \ge 0$ is a hypothesis, not a deduction.** It follows from $\lVert f'(t)\rVert_2 \le M$ at any single $t \in (a,b)$, and $(a,b)$ is nonempty here because $a<b$; it is stated anyway so that the conclusion reads as a genuine bound, in the style of [[cor-bounded-derivative-implies-lipschitz]].
--
